<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!--
    RDR-156 Decision 5 (bead nexus-ubnwk) — one-time backfill of
    nexus.document_aspects.doc_id for legacy rows that predate the
    fk-001-2 nullable-FK conversion.

    nexus-x1de2 (2026-08-28) made every NEW aspect upsert stamp doc_id
    go-forward; this changeset repairs the PRE-EXISTING population so the
    new search_aspect_scoped_&lt;dim&gt; function (vectors-008, this same
    bead) has something to join against on day one. Join identity is
    doc_id -&gt; catalog_documents.tumbler, the exact shape the shipped
    combined-query functions already use — an exact source_uri match is
    the only safe correlation key available (aspects has no tumbler
    column of its own): a legacy row whose source_uri does not exactly
    equal a live, non-alias, non-tombstoned catalog document's source_uri
    stays doc_id IS NULL and simply never matches the new function's join
    — correct, not a bug.

    REAL COVERAGE (round-1 review disclosure, substantive-critic Critical
    — this is NOT "some legacy notes predate URI normalization" edge-case
    drift, it is a structural, permanent split by corpus): this backfill
    only attributes rows whose document_aspects.source_uri is BYTE-FOR-
    BYTE EQUAL to the catalog document's own source_uri. That holds for
    file-keyed corpora (code__/docs__/rdr__, source_uri = file://...) —
    both producers key off the same file path. For knowledge__
    collections it structurally does NOT hold: the catalog registers
    those documents' source_uri from TITLE
    (src/nexus/catalog/store_hook.py:286, uri_for(collection, title)),
    while the aspect extractor's source_uri is built from source_path
    (src/nexus/aspect_readers.py:172, chroma://{collection}/
    {source_path}) — often itself a content-hash string, not a
    human-legible path. Title and source_path are two DIFFERENT identity
    fields for the same document family, not the same field spelled two
    ways, so an exact match essentially never occurs there. The large
    majority of knowledge__ document_aspects rows (the dominant aspects
    corpus) therefore stay doc_id NULL after this walk and are invisible
    to search_aspect_scoped until they are RE-EXTRACTED under
    nexus-x1de2's go-forward stamping (which keys off the extraction
    queue's own doc_id, not source_uri, so it does not retroactively fix
    these legacy rows either). A follow-up gap-fill for the knowledge__
    family is tracked as nexus-bocft.

    FORCE-RLS toggle (tests/test_changelog_rls_lint.py "Approved Shape 1"):
    this UPDATE is tenant-spanning (repairs every tenant's rows in one
    pass, matching fk-001-2's own tenant-spanning cleanup UPDATE) but,
    unlike fk-001-2, has no immediately-valid ADD CONSTRAINT to fall back
    on as a same-changeset backstop if the write silently no-ops under
    FORCE ROW LEVEL SECURITY. nexus_admin (Liquibase's migration role,
    src/nexus/db/pg_provision.py) is NOSUPERUSER/NOBYPASSRLS and owns
    both tables, so FORCE applies to it too, and no nexus.tenant GUC is
    set during migration (TenantScope only sets it per-request, never at
    boot) — a bare UPDATE would match ZERO rows for EVERY tenant, silently.
    Toggling NO FORCE for the statement's duration (catalog-014-0's exact
    precedent: "BOTH tables need the FORCE toggle... toggling only the
    target table leaves the join yielding zero rows for the non-bypass
    owner") makes nexus_admin's ordinary table-owner RLS bypass apply, so
    the UPDATE actually runs across every tenant, then FORCE is restored
    on both tables before the changeset ends. Both nexus.document_aspects
    (the UPDATE's target) and nexus.catalog_documents (joined via the FROM
    clause) are toggled, in that order; restored in reverse (LIFO), same
    as catalog-014-0.

    Idempotent: doc_id IS NULL in the WHERE clause means a re-run only
    ever touches rows a prior run left unmatched (there are none — a
    matched row's doc_id is no longer NULL) or new legacy rows that
    appeared since (there are none once nexus-x1de2's go-forward stamp is
    live). No-op rollback: this is a repair of existing data, not new
    data — a rolled-back cluster simply has doc_id NULL again for rows
    this backfill would re-stamp identically on the next apply, mirroring
    catalog-014-0's own "derived denormalization, nothing to restore"
    rollback rationale.

    Proof: AspectDocIdBackfillTest exercises this exact changeset's SQL
    against a genuine PgContainerHelper.startDedicated() cluster carrying
    a pre-seeded NULL-doc_id row with a matching catalog source_uri and
    asserts the walk stamps it (see that test's own header for why a
    dedicated, not shared-template, container is required).
-->
<databaseChangeLog
    xmlns="http://www.liquibase.org/xml/ns/dbchangelog"
    xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance"
    xsi:schemaLocation="http://www.liquibase.org/xml/ns/dbchangelog
        http://www.liquibase.org/xml/ns/dbchangelog/dbchangelog-4.4.xsd">

    <changeSet id="aspects-004-1" author="nexus-ubnwk">
        <comment>One-time backfill: nexus.document_aspects.doc_id = catalog_documents.tumbler for legacy rows matched by exact source_uri (RDR-156 Decision 5, search_aspect_scoped's join precondition).</comment>
        <sql splitStatements="true" stripComments="false">
ALTER TABLE nexus.document_aspects  NO FORCE ROW LEVEL SECURITY;
ALTER TABLE nexus.catalog_documents NO FORCE ROW LEVEL SECURITY;

UPDATE nexus.document_aspects a
   SET doc_id = d.tumbler
  FROM nexus.catalog_documents d
 WHERE a.doc_id IS NULL
   AND d.tenant_id = a.tenant_id
   AND d.source_uri = a.source_uri
   AND d.source_uri &lt;&gt; ''
   AND d.alias_of = ''
   AND d.deleted_at IS NULL;

ALTER TABLE nexus.catalog_documents FORCE ROW LEVEL SECURITY;
ALTER TABLE nexus.document_aspects  FORCE ROW LEVEL SECURITY;
        </sql>
        <rollback>
            <!-- Repair of pre-existing data, not new data: nothing to restore.
                 A rolled-back cluster has doc_id NULL again for rows this
                 backfill would re-stamp identically on the next apply
                 (catalog-014-0's own "derived denormalization" rollback
                 rationale). -->
        </rollback>
    </changeSet>

</databaseChangeLog>
